WEBVTT

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.320
North Africa!

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.320
North Africa.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.320
High in Morocco's Atlas Mountains,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.320
Barbary macaques shiver in the icy cedars.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.320
The ancestors of these monkeys fled here from a disaster that overwhelmed their homeland.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:12.320
Now trapped in this isolated corner of Africa,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.320
there's no going back to the land farther south.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:35.320
Even in this snowy refuge, there's a reminder of what drove them here.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:41.320
The unbridled power of the African side.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:49.320
The unbridled power of the African sun.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:58.320
Under its intense gaze, the snow can't last for long.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:09.320
Melt water should bring life to the lowlands.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:16.320
Hundreds of torrents cascade southwards,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:25.320
but each is flowing towards extinction.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:37.320
Just 200 miles south of the mountains, the rivers are vaporized.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:49.320
Life has been burnt off the land.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:55.320
This was the apocalypse from which the Barbary macaques fled.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:14.320
The sudden and unstoppable advance of the greatest desert on the planet.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.320
The Sahara transformed North Africa.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.320
Today, it covers an area the size of the United States,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:32.320
one third of the entire African continent.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:44.320
This is one of the hottest places on Earth.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:50.320
The merciless sun, a colossal 15 million degree nuclear reactor,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:57.320
blasted life from the surface of the land.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.320
It still wreaks havoc.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:28.320
A faint breath of wind can be the beginning of disaster.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.020
The

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:55.320
nomads tell of entire villages being engulfed.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:24.320
The mines disappearing and people buried alive inside their tents.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:51.320
A

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:58.320
sandstorm can be a thousand miles across.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:07.320
It seems miraculous that anything can survive such devastation.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.320
The Saharan apocalypse wiped out many creatures,

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:23.320
but today some still cling on in the lands around the margins of the great desert.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.320
It's very dry here.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:38.320
Some years, the rains fail entirely.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.320
A lone Grevy's zebra.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:54.320
He weighs close to half a ton and could go for three days without drinking.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:07:05.320
Like the macaques, his forbearers were refugees from the advancing Sahara.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:13.320
The land is scrubby and dry.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.320
This stallion has claimed it as his own.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:32.320
He's been waiting months for visitors, female visitors.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:40.320
If they like his territory, they might stay a while.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:58.320
It's his first chance to mate for a very long time.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:02.320
Hardly a success.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:09.320
Perhaps his visitors are looking for a more impressive partner.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:13.320
There's another setback.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:25.320
The females were being followed. A posse of young males. Everyone a rival.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:54.320
It's time to separate the men from the boys.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:09:21.320
One by one, the stallion sees them off.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:26.320
The females had ringside seats.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:36.320
And his prowess has not gone unnoted.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:56.320
Matizmo gives way to Kentonus.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:14.320
Around here, you have to take every opportunity. Be it for food, for water, or for mates.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:22.320
Female grevys are a fickle bunch. The herd have decided to move on.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.320
All of them.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.320
The stallion may never see them again.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:35.320
But there's a chance that one is now carrying his foal.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:48.320
In this harsh land, that must count as a triumph.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:54.320
The sun's power cannot, however, reach far underground.

00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:02.320
Below, in stark contrast, conditions are stable and tolerable.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:10.320
And home to one of the planet's strangest mammals.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:19.320
Meet the naked mole rats.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:26.320
These saber-toothed sausages wouldn't last a day in the desert.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.320
Special filming tunnels allow us to see how well adapted they are to the subterranean life.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:41.320
They can run equally well in both directions, so tight space is no problem.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.320
They have lost their fur.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:55.320
And most bizarrely, they live in social colonies, much like termites or ants.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:11.320
After time spent digging, the workers come together to relax.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:17.320
But one here is very different from all the rest.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:30.320
Their queen, twice as heavy as her subjects, and not afraid to throw her weight around.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:39.320
She is the mother of every worker in the colony and exists in a near continuous state of pregnancy.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:50.320
Even now, two dozen babies are pulsating within her swollen belly.

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:56.320
Just occasionally, one of her brood is raised differently.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:02.320
A daughter becomes a princess.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:15.320
Until now, this youngster's enjoyed a lazy, privileged life. But not for much longer.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:25.320
She has a destiny to fulfill.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:37.320
The surface is a place where no naked mole rat can survive for long.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:51.320
But a princess will risk everything to search for a partner.

00:13:51.320 --> 00:14:01.320
The quest is urgent.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:13.320
There's an enticing smell in the air.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:29.320
The seductive scent draws her downwards to safety.

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:35.320
She's sniffed out a partner.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:44.320
The old man is alone and eager to start a new colony in his lonely burrow.

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:58.320
Two months later, the princess has become a queen and a new tyranny begins.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:12.320
Tough though they are, such refugees living on the edges of North Africa cannot survive in the heart of the Sahara.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:25.320
And yet here in southern Nigeria, there are creatures preparing to journey right across the center of that great desert.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:35.320
The barn swallows.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:41.320
They spent the winter roosting in a forest of elephant grass.

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:49.320
But now it's time for them to leave.

00:15:49.320 --> 00:16:14.320
All two million of them.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:20.320
They're tiny, each weighing the same as a couple of one pound coins.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:27.320
Yet the journey to their breeding grounds in Europe is over 3000 miles long.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:34.320
Ahead of them lies a vast death trap.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:53.320
The Sahara is too large to go around. The swallows have no choice but to meet it head on.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:17:01.320
It will take one of nature's greatest feats of navigation to cross this lifeless wasteland.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:17.320
A wilderness that stretches not just to the horizon, but almost beyond imagination.

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:28.320
It's an immense blank space on the map.

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:37.320
In spite of the Sahara's reputation, less than one fifth of it is sand.

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:51.320
The rest is stone and wind scarred rock.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:18:00.320
The sun not only bakes the land, it warps its appearance.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:11.320
The superheated air rising upward from the desert surface distorts the distant scene.

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:22.320
A reflection of the sky shimmers on the sands. A mirage.

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:29.320
The sun is an illusionist.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:44.320
To thirsty travelers, a mirage can resemble a lake, which agonizingly recedes as it's approached.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:19:02.320
And swaying camels coming to the rescue transform into dry, spiny acacia trees.

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:18.320
To cross this confused, shimmering landscape, many swallows will need to find real water amongst the mirages.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:30.320
Even in the Sahara, rain does sometimes fall, and that is sufficient for plants to survive, providing they have the right adaptations.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:42.320
Rising from the sand, a dried out ball of twigs.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:20:02.320
And strong winds, it can travel.

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:09.320
This plant may have been dead for a hundred years.

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:23.320
Its name suggests that all is not lost, for this is a resurrection plant.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:42.320
Around here, rain might only fall once or twice a year. But if you're searching for decades, that might be enough.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:50.320
Dead limbs absorb water and unfurl in a matter of minutes.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:21:01.320
But the resurrection plant needs one more miracle.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:27.320
Rain must fall on its branches before they dry out and close up again.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:49.320
Rain must fall on its branches before they dry out and close up again.

00:21:49.320 --> 00:22:17.320
Within hours, shoots emerge.

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:35.320
In just a few weeks, they flower a developed seeds of their own.

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:46.320
Then, before they can grow any larger, the sun kills them.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:58.320
But their seeds live on, ready for when the rains return, even if that is a century from now.

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:01.320
North Africa wasn't always so brutal.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:12.320
Scattered across the Sahara, a glimpses of life before the apocalypse swept over the land.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:24.320
In the north, a petrified forest, trees turned to stone, remains from a far distant, wetter past.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:35.320
White sediments in the heart of the Sahara are the dried-out remains of what was once the world's largest lake.

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:47.320
In the east, ruined cities hark back to a time of plenty.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:57.320
And here, deep inside Libya, is Mesak Setafet.

00:23:57.320 --> 00:24:07.320
Carved here are hundreds of images of animals, all drawn from life.

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:15.320
Ghosts from a greener time.

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:24.320
Remarkably, a remnant of this old North Africa survives.

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:34.320
Buhetma in Tunisia is sustained by mountain rains.

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:50.320
It's a relic of the savanna that once carpeted North Africa.

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:57.320
The vast grassland vanished when a shift in the Earth's orbit drove the rain south.

00:24:57.320 --> 00:25:08.320
And in perhaps only a matter of centuries, the Sahara Desert overwhelmed North Africa.

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:15.320
The evidence suggests this took place around 6,000 years ago, in evolutionary terms at no time at all.

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:26.320
And life has had little chance to adapt to this new world.

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:50.320
Only a few tough specialists can cope with life amongst the dunes.

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:59.320
Camels are sometimes called ships of the desert, but like the swallows, they're really only visitors here.

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:11.320
These ships can certainly cross the Sahara, but even they can't make their home in the harshest places.

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:16.320
Left to wander the desert by themselves, camels would not survive.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:28.320
They depend on their human navigators to find oases and wells.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:51.320
Saharan folklore is full of tales of caravans that missed a well by a few hundred meters and disappeared.

00:26:51.320 --> 00:27:00.320
This is the White Desert in Egypt.

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:12.320
The landscape is littered with giant chalk pillars carved by innumerable sandstorms.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:19.320
This glaring white oven is lethally hot.

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:25.320
Food here is almost non-existent.

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:29.320
But there's a rare gift from a passing camel.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:35.320
The smell has lured dung beetles from miles around.

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:45.320
For them, this is manna from heaven.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:53.320
One dung bull could provide enough food to last this female beetle the rest of her life.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:56.320
But she has a problem.

00:27:56.320 --> 00:28:00.320
To keep it fresh, she must bury it in moist ground.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:11.320
And that's not easy to find.

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:20.320
The temperature has already risen 10 degrees.

00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:24.320
This lizard avoids the roasting sand.

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:37.320
Only 30 centimeters above the surface, it's significantly cooler.

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:49.320
The reverse pushing technique is certainly the fastest way to keep the ball rolling.

00:28:49.320 --> 00:29:00.320
But it does have one drawback.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:07.320
You can't see where you're going.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.320
Disaster.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:21.320
Stuck between two dunes.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:49.320
The dung bull is twice her weight, but the urge to keep pushing is inextinguishable.

00:29:49.320 --> 00:30:15.320
Now it's 41 degrees Celsius.

00:30:15.320 --> 00:30:22.320
Soon she'll be baked alive.

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:39.320
Her survival instinct in the end overrides her love for dung.

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:46.320
Much of the Sahara is uninhabitable, but there are rare places where there is some possibility of survival.

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:55.320
Places where by strange chance there is water.

00:30:55.320 --> 00:31:12.320
Wow, Aounamos is an extinct volcano. From space, it's a remote black scar on the Libyan Sahara.

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:20.320
Yet there are other colors here. Colors rarely seen on the desert floor.

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:36.320
Blue and green.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:44.320
Rain fell thousands of years ago when the Sahara was green and percolated deep into the ground.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:52.320
And here water from this vast ancient reservoir rises to the surface.

00:31:52.320 --> 00:32:03.320
These pools offer another glimpse of the Sahara's past.

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:14.320
Wherever there's water in North Africa, living relics from this wetter time have a chance to cling on.

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:24.320
This oasis is fed by a hot volcanic spring.

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:36.320
Slightly away from the stream of near boiling water, it's cooler and fish can live. These are tilapia.

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:48.320
Hatchlings stick close to their mother. There are other dangers here beside the scalding water.

00:32:48.320 --> 00:33:01.320
Particularly at night.

00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:13.320
The crocodiles are stealthy.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:22.320
The tilapia are almost blind in the darkness.

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:33.320
In panic, they all leap to escape the hunter's approach.

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:45.320
But this female can't abandon her brood.

00:33:45.320 --> 00:34:14.320
The crocodiles won't be thwarted. They too can leap.

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:42.320
With first light, the crocodiles lose the element of surprise and the battle is over for now.

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:51.320
The mother fish has survived, but where are her young?

00:34:51.320 --> 00:34:59.320
All present and correct. They spent the whole night sheltering in her mouth.

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:11.320
The contest will be repeated at sunset. There is nowhere else to go.

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:25.320
Oasis are always sought by desert travelers, but not all are as they seem.

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:35.320
This is the great Ubaari San Sea in the heart of the Sahara.

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:43.320
These swallows have traveled one and a half thousand miles since they left Nigeria.

00:35:43.320 --> 00:36:00.320
Their superb powers of navigation will eventually guide them to Europe, but now they and other thirsty migrants need to find a speck of blue amidst this ocean of sand.

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:13.320
And here it is, Umm Al Mar.

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:37.320
Here too, ancient groundwater wells up to the surface.

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:51.320
But the birds need to be careful for the sun has played a terrible trick.

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:57.320
This oasis is poisonous.

00:36:57.320 --> 00:37:06.320
Intensive operation over thousands of years has left the water saltier than the sea.

00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:17.320
In an attempt to underline the horror, the place is infested by vast swarms of flies.

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:25.320
But this plague is a bird's salvation.

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:35.320
The flies are filled with fresh water filtered from the brine.

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:51.320
So like a desert wanderer squeezing a drink from a cactus, the birds get all the water they need from the flies bodies.

00:37:51.320 --> 00:38:14.320
More and more migrants join in.

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:18.320
This is the birds only stopover.

00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:27.320
It gives them enough fuel to escape from the Sahara and Africa.

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:37.320
Away from an oasis, it seems remarkable that anything can live at all.

00:38:37.320 --> 00:38:44.320
The temperature of the sands can exceed 70 degrees Celsius.

00:38:44.320 --> 00:38:54.320
It's not the slightest trace of water left at the surface.

00:38:54.320 --> 00:39:23.320
When that happens, the Sahara itself cries out.

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:41.320
Billions of sliding grains generate a hum that echoes across miles of empty desert.

00:39:41.320 --> 00:40:02.320
These are the Sahara's legendary singing dunes.

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:19.320
Over time, these avalanches add up.

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:27.320
If you watch the dunes for long enough, something remarkable is revealed.

00:40:27.320 --> 00:40:40.320
One and a half years flash past in a matter of seconds.

00:40:40.320 --> 00:40:51.320
On this timescale, the dunes are like a stormy sea.

00:40:51.320 --> 00:41:03.320
An unstoppable tsunami of sand.

00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:15.320
In this immense, ever-shifting landscape, it's easy to see how a lost traveler could succumb to what's been called the Sahara's only endemic disease.

00:41:15.320 --> 00:41:37.320
The Sahara's only endemic disease is madness.

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:49.320
Can you imagine what it feels like in the African desert when the sun is at its fiercest?

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:54.320
It's approaching midday.

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:57.320
A fringe toed lizard is hungry.

00:41:57.320 --> 00:42:06.320
He's on a stakeout.

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:11.320
The sun's rays reflect some of the sun's rays.

00:42:11.320 --> 00:42:19.320
Nevertheless, the heat is almost unbearable.

00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:35.320
His prey hasn't left home all day.

00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:44.320
The desert is the last animal still out on the dunes.

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:48.320
But even he can't take it anymore.

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:55.320
To survive longer, you'd need a space suit.

00:42:55.320 --> 00:43:02.320
And in a way, that's what these insects have.

00:43:02.320 --> 00:43:06.320
Silver ants, armored skin, reflects light.

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:13.320
They can tolerate temperatures that would kill any other land animal.

00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:27.320
Even so, they can only survive for less than 10 minutes in the midday sun.

00:43:27.320 --> 00:43:30.320
Time is precious.

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:43.320
The ants race to find food as soon as their predators go to ground.

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:51.320
They can't afford to waste a second getting lost, so they spin to take a bearing from the sun.

00:43:51.320 --> 00:44:05.320
They log every change of direction, every footstep, in order to know exactly where they are and where their nest lies.

00:44:05.320 --> 00:44:10.320
Only four minutes to spare, and they found a victim of heat stroke.

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:15.320
A meal.

00:44:15.320 --> 00:44:33.320
But it's going to take a monumental effort to get it home.

00:44:33.320 --> 00:44:48.320
Three minutes to go, and they're nearing their maximum temperature, an astounding 53 degrees Celsius.

00:44:48.320 --> 00:45:02.320
But there are already casualties.

00:45:02.320 --> 00:45:05.320
One minute left, and they're not going to make it.

00:45:32.320 --> 00:45:59.320
The silver ant is the hardest of all desert inhabitants.

00:45:59.320 --> 00:46:10.320
Even so, it can only survive outside in the middle of the day for a matter of minutes.

00:46:10.320 --> 00:46:17.320
Now the desert belongs to the sun alone.

00:46:17.320 --> 00:46:32.320
The sun has scorched life from the Sahara, and yet the vast desert it created is a source of life half a world away.

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:46.320
The advancing Sahara vaporized the world's largest lake, leaving behind the silvery remains of countless microscopic algae.

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:57.320
In winter, the wind carries away 700,000 tons of this mineral-rich dust every day.

00:46:57.320 --> 00:47:12.320
It blows from here all the way to South America, where, astonishingly, it fertilizes the Amazon rainforest.

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:28.320
A striking demonstration of the reach of this mighty continent.

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:33.320
Throughout its long history, Africa has influenced the entire planet.

00:47:33.320 --> 00:48:00.320
It was the cradle of a remarkable array of land animals that spread across the globe, and of course, it was the ancestral home of all of us.

00:48:03.320 --> 00:48:24.320
This is the tale of two of the Africa team's most challenging desert expeditions.

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:31.320
One focused on a miniscule creature with an incredible turn of speed.

00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:38.320
The other, on a subject so slow, to film it in action would take years.

00:48:38.320 --> 00:48:49.320
In both cases, the Sahara would push crews to the limit in pursuit of the perfect shot.

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:57.320
In Tunisia, the mission is to capture footage of moving sand dunes, something that's never been tried like this before.

00:48:57.320 --> 00:49:06.320
The problem is that because the genes move so slowly, we're going to have to leave cameras here for about 20 months, and that just means there's a huge potential for things to go wrong.

00:49:06.320 --> 00:49:12.320
And with filmmaking, if something can go wrong, it usually will.

00:49:12.320 --> 00:49:21.320
Two local shepherds, Amor and Lusa, have volunteered to tend the equipment full-time.

00:49:21.320 --> 00:49:29.320
The camera tower will be the tallest structure for as far as the eye can see.

00:49:29.320 --> 00:49:35.320
And there are three other cameras at lower angles.

00:49:35.320 --> 00:49:40.320
All this toil will yield surprisingly scant results.

00:49:40.320 --> 00:49:44.320
They've programmed the cameras to take one photo every day.

00:49:44.320 --> 00:49:48.320
That's only 365 photographs a year,

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:52.320
and you run it at normal speed just over 14 seconds.

00:49:52.320 --> 00:49:59.320
It's taken longer to explain what's going to happen, and the end result will actually be...

00:49:59.320 --> 00:50:05.320
The cameras are left to the mercy of the sun, wind and sand.

00:50:05.320 --> 00:50:12.320
In the meantime, crews are shooting all across North Africa.

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:19.320
In Egypt, the challenge is to get into the world of the most heat-tolerant desert animal, the silver ant.

00:50:19.320 --> 00:50:22.320
They're really small, they move really fast.

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:27.320
Like, you're not too sure if you've seen an ant.

00:50:27.320 --> 00:50:32.320
The crew have three weeks to gather the footage they need.

00:50:32.320 --> 00:50:42.320
They're going to try a tracking shot on this ant nest, moving forward towards it as the ants pour out of the hole in their millions.

00:50:42.320 --> 00:50:48.320
Not only are these insects super fast, they also keep antisocial hours.

00:50:48.320 --> 00:50:52.320
The thing is, we need to be out here in the middle of the day to film these ants.

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:58.320
They don't do what they do, and it's nice and cool at seven, eight o'clock in the morning.

00:50:58.320 --> 00:51:03.320
I can't remember ever being in a place where the wind was so relentless and the temperatures were so high.

00:51:07.320 --> 00:51:10.320
The insufferable heat is not the only problem.

00:51:10.320 --> 00:51:12.320
Dangers are everywhere.

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:14.320
Ooh!

00:51:14.320 --> 00:51:18.320
There's a really fat scorpion. It's really big.

00:51:18.320 --> 00:51:20.320
One, two, three.

00:51:20.320 --> 00:51:25.320
This might kill.

00:51:25.320 --> 00:51:26.320
Yeah?

00:51:26.320 --> 00:51:27.320
Yeah.

00:51:27.320 --> 00:51:31.320
It's big and they have a lot of poison in his dark thing.

00:51:31.320 --> 00:51:32.320
What, that thing there?

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:33.320
It hit!

00:51:33.320 --> 00:51:34.320
Oh my God!

00:51:34.320 --> 00:51:36.320
Don't touch it!

00:51:36.320 --> 00:51:43.320
The scorpion will be released far, far away from the camp in a shady spot.

00:51:43.320 --> 00:51:48.320
No such luck for the team. They are back to work in the midday sun.

00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:54.320
This is too much. This is crazy. This is crazy.

00:51:54.320 --> 00:52:00.320
Indeed, the heat seems to have given Cat and Warwick a touch of Saharan madness.

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:09.320
The plan is to do an experiment to find out how fast these little ants can run.

00:52:09.320 --> 00:52:17.320
So we're going to lay this along the floor and hopefully an ant will run alongside it and we can film it at high speed.

00:52:17.320 --> 00:52:27.320
And then from that, calculate their speed and perhaps try and relate it to how fast that would be for a human like me.

00:52:27.320 --> 00:52:32.320
Silver ants are expert navigators using the angle of the sun to calculate their position.

00:52:32.320 --> 00:52:38.320
But for our team, even basic mental tasks are becoming a challenge.

00:52:38.320 --> 00:52:41.320
Count the second. See one running and then count the second.

00:52:41.320 --> 00:52:43.320
It's quite easy for us to count the second, isn't it?

00:52:43.320 --> 00:52:46.320
No, it's one.

00:52:46.320 --> 00:52:52.320
One, yeah.

00:52:52.320 --> 00:52:56.320
There he goes.

00:52:56.320 --> 00:52:58.320
He's gone 10 centimetres in four seconds.

00:52:58.320 --> 00:52:59.320
Yeah.

00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:04.320
But we're running at 500 frames a second, which is 20 times normal times.

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:05.320
Yeah.

00:53:05.320 --> 00:53:12.320
So in fact, he's covered those 10 centimetres in...

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:15.320
We know that he does half a metre in one second.

00:53:15.320 --> 00:53:18.320
Half a metre per second, yeah.

00:53:18.320 --> 00:53:20.320
50 centimetres in one second.

00:53:20.320 --> 00:53:21.320
Yeah.

00:53:21.320 --> 00:53:22.320
Roughly.

00:53:22.320 --> 00:53:26.320
So how many body lengths is that?

00:53:26.320 --> 00:53:30.320
He's maybe doing five body lengths a second if he's two metres tall like I am.

00:53:30.320 --> 00:53:31.320
Are you?

00:53:31.320 --> 00:53:40.320
Yeah, that's how much more than a normal man I am.

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:45.320
Eventually, the duo decide that if the silver ants were our size,

00:53:45.320 --> 00:53:52.320
they'd be doing 280 miles an hour.

00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:56.320
They're one of the fastest sprinters in the animal kingdom.

00:53:56.320 --> 00:53:58.320
No wonder we've been struggling to film.

00:53:58.320 --> 00:54:02.320
It does explain a few things.

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:07.320
Antletics.

00:54:07.320 --> 00:54:10.320
Knowing the exact speed of the ants is all well and good,

00:54:10.320 --> 00:54:16.320
but there's still a great deal of work to be done before the shoot finishes.

00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:20.320
However, in Tunisia, there's no shortage of time,

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:30.320
and hopefully no news is good news as far as Amur and Nasser are concerned.

00:54:30.320 --> 00:54:39.320
The final week in Egypt, and the crew seem to be adapting to life in the oven.

00:54:39.320 --> 00:54:47.320
Practice is making perfect, and the sequence is coming together.

00:54:47.320 --> 00:54:49.320
I think we've got some lovely shots.

00:54:49.320 --> 00:54:51.320
Every single shot has been really hard-earned.

00:54:51.320 --> 00:54:55.320
But getting down in the ants' world is now taking its toll on the kit.

00:54:55.320 --> 00:55:02.320
It's running to stand still, the business of blowing dust off these things.

00:55:02.320 --> 00:55:03.320
Oh, no.

00:55:03.320 --> 00:55:06.320
It's got dust in it.

00:55:06.320 --> 00:55:10.320
Oh, crunch.

00:55:10.320 --> 00:55:12.320
I think these ants are stunning-looking.

00:55:12.320 --> 00:55:15.320
Near impossible to film, I think, because of the speed they had.

00:55:15.320 --> 00:55:21.320
But, you know, I've come to love them over the days and weeks.

00:55:21.320 --> 00:55:27.320
With a sequence in the bag, Warwick wants the final traditional sunset shot.

00:55:27.320 --> 00:55:33.320
It's the best time of day to film sunsets in the evening.

00:55:33.320 --> 00:55:35.320
That's experience that tells me that.

00:55:35.320 --> 00:55:38.320
I've been doing this for years. You learn these things.

00:55:38.320 --> 00:55:41.320
Thanks to Warwick's experience, including sunsets,

00:55:41.320 --> 00:55:50.320
he and Kat have captured the extraordinary life of the speedy silver ant.

00:55:50.320 --> 00:55:57.320
Over a year later, in Tunisia, it's time to take down these sand dune cameras.

00:55:57.320 --> 00:55:59.320
Bonjour.

00:55:59.320 --> 00:56:01.320
Nice to meet you.

00:56:01.320 --> 00:56:03.320
Hi. Hi. Hi again.

00:56:03.320 --> 00:56:05.320
So how's it been? Has it been good?

00:56:05.320 --> 00:56:10.320
It's OK, but two days ago we have a little bit smaller problem.

00:56:10.320 --> 00:56:13.320
After surviving 600 days in the desert,

00:56:13.320 --> 00:56:20.320
the small problem is that the cameras have been vandalised.

00:56:20.320 --> 00:56:23.320
I'm really hot and bothered now. It's 40 degrees.

00:56:23.320 --> 00:56:26.320
And someone smashed the cameras.

00:56:26.320 --> 00:56:30.320
Not been a good start to the day, to be honest.

00:56:30.320 --> 00:56:33.320
There's no doubt the dunes have moved,

00:56:33.320 --> 00:56:40.320
but the question is whether the equipment has survived.

00:56:40.320 --> 00:56:42.320
That is amazing, the camera's still here.

00:56:42.320 --> 00:56:46.320
I guess maybe it just took them so long to get through the tough on plastic

00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:48.320
that they felt that they'd made so much noise

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:50.320
they were worried about the guards coming

00:56:50.320 --> 00:56:53.320
because they only sleep a couple of hundred metres away.

00:56:53.320 --> 00:57:00.320
After almost two years of waiting, it's the moment of truth.

00:57:00.320 --> 00:57:05.320
I'm going to find out whether or not the cameras have actually recorded anything.

00:57:05.320 --> 00:57:12.320
It's just hugely stressful because it's never been done before.

00:57:12.320 --> 00:57:16.320
The footage is a surreal window into a secret world,

00:57:16.320 --> 00:57:20.320
the private life of a sand dune.

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:22.320
Yeah.

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:25.320
Well, what's this?

00:57:25.320 --> 00:57:28.320
A moon.

